Sure, here's an example of how to generate an automatically generated table of contents (TOC) in LaTeX:

1. First, add the `toc` package to your document preamble:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{tocloft} % for customizing the TOC

\begin{document}
```

2. Use the `\tableofcontents` command after your document title, to generate the TOC:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{tocloft} % for customizing the TOC

\begin{document}
\title{My Document}
\maketitle
\tableofcontents
```

3. Add section headings (e.g., `\section`, `\subsection`, etc.) throughout your document to generate entries for the TOC.

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{tocloft} % for customizing the TOC

\begin{document}
\title{My Document}
\maketitle
\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}
This is the introduction.

\subsection{Background}
This is some background information.

\section{Methodology}
This is the methodology section.

\subsection{Data Collection}
This is the data collection subsection.

\subsection{Data Analysis}
This is the data analysis subsection.

\section{Results}
These are the results.

\section{Discussion}
This is the discussion section.

\end{document}
```

4. Customize the TOC with the `tocloft` package, if needed. For example, you can change the font size, spacing, and label formatting:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{tocloft} % for customizing the TOC

\setlength{\cftsecnumwidth}{2em} % Change section number width
\renewcommand{\cftsecfont}{\normalfont} % Change section font
\renewcommand{\cftsecpagefont}{\normalfont} % Change section page number font
\renewcommand{\cftsubsecfont}{\normalfont} % Change subsection font
