To draw a chessboard in LaTeX, you can use the `chessboard` package, which provides commands to easily create a chessboard with a given FEN notation.

First, you need to add the `chessboard` package to your LaTeX document by adding the following line to the preamble:

```
\usepackage{chessboard}
```

Then, you can use the `ChessBoard` command to draw a chessboard with the given FEN notation. Here's an example:

```
\documentclass{article}

\usepackage{chessboard}

\begin{document}

\setboard[FEN="rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR"]

\begin{center}
  \ChessBoard
\end{center}

\end{document}
```

In this example, we're setting the board using the FEN notation `rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR`, which represents a standard starting position for a chess game.

The `\setboard` command sets the initial state of the board, while the `\ChessBoard` command generates the actual chessboard with the pieces placed according to the FEN notation.

You can customize the appearance of the chessboard by using various options for the `\setboard` command. For example, you can change the font, size, and color of the board and pieces by adding the following lines to the preamble:

```
\usepackage{xcolor}

\definecolor{chessboard}{RGB}{235,235,235}
\definecolor{chesspiece}{RGB}{100,100,100}

\setinformationfont{\small\bfseries}
\setboardfont{\small\bfseries}
\setlightsquarefont{\color{chesspiece}}
\setdarksquarefont{\color{chesspiece}}
\setboardsize{10cm}
```

The `xcolor` package is needed for defining custom colors.

